course: dictionary
lesson: 9831
title: Swerve and sudden changes
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Swerve is a word with more than one meaning. As a verb, it means to change direction suddenly, often to avoid hitting something. «The car swerved to miss the dog.» The words «to miss the dog» are the clue: the car moved sideways quickly because it was in danger.
explain: Ask yourself what would fit. Try «turned quickly» in the sentence instead: «The car turned quickly to miss the dog.» It still makes sense, so swerve means change direction suddenly.
explain: Swerve can also be a noun, meaning the sudden change itself, as in «make a swerve». In British slang, «I swerved that party» means I avoided it on purpose. The idea is the same: you move away from something quickly.
fact: Swerve means to change direction suddenly, especially to avoid hitting something.
fact: A swerve is a sudden change of direction.
fact: «The driver only just managed to swerve» means the driver avoided the danger at the very last second.
fact: Trams and trains cannot swerve, because they run on rails: «a train can never swerve round anything on the line.»
fact: Car makers in Sweden test cars with a sharp swerve called the moose test.
exercise: «The car swerved to miss the dog.» What does swerved mean? | Went backwards slowly | *Changed direction suddenly | Stopped completely and waited | Honked loudly at the dog
exercise: «A tram runs on rails and cannot swerve.» Why can a tram not swerve? | *It runs on rails, so it cannot change direction | It is too quiet to be heard | It has too many passengers | It is powered by a petrol engine
exercise: What is a swerve, as a noun? | *A sudden change of direction | A slow drive down a long road | A parked car at the kerb | A loud horn on a bus
test: Mia says «I swerved that party». What does she mean? | She went to the party and danced all night | She lost the invitation to the party | She was pushed off the road by a car | *She avoided the party on purpose
test: «A lorry is so long it swings wide on corners.» Why does a lorry need a long way to stop? | *It is so big and heavy that it cannot stop quickly | It only drives at night | It is too quiet for other drivers to hear | It runs on rails and cannot swerve
exam: A player makes a swerve past a defender. What does this mean? | *She changed direction suddenly to get around the defender | She kicked the ball out of the pitch | She ran straight into the defender | She stood still and let the defender pass
